<TopicPage slug="subjects/finance/advanced/options-and-derivatives">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Derivatives are <strong>contracts whose value derives from something else</strong> — stocks,
    rates, commodities, even weather. Options are the core: rights to buy or sell at a set price,
    giving asymmetric payoffs that no plain stock can replicate. Used well they hedge risk;
    used with leverage they create it — derivatives are tools, and the 2008 crisis was a
    tools-in-the-dark story.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="calls, puts, payoff shapes, and the pricing formula" widget="equationStep" config={optCfg} />

  <h2>What derivatives are actually for</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Hedging:</strong> an airline buys fuel options to cap jet-fuel costs; a farmer sells crop futures; a fund buys index puts before volatility. Shifting risk to those paid to hold it.</li>
    <li><strong>Price discovery:</strong> option prices reveal the market's implied volatility and probability expectations — information no spot market provides.</li>
    <li><strong>Speculation with leverage:</strong> small premium controls large position — gains multiplied, losses can exceed deposits. The leverage is why notional values dwarf the real economy.</li>
    <li><strong>The systemic lesson (2008):</strong> credit default swaps and mortgage securities were sensible individually; opacity, chain-linkage and leverage made them systemic. Notional ~$600T notional vs ~$100T world GDP — the plumbing matters as much as the maths.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Black & Scholes (1973); Hull, Options, Futures and Other Derivatives; McDonald, Derivatives Markets.</p>
</TopicPage>
